# Grandma Ruth 93 · ANGLE: test story (grandmother pretends to be going deaf and hears what her family really says)

sc("""Sweetheart, when I turned seventy-two, my doctor told me my hearing was perfect. But I came home and told my family I was going deaf. I bought a hearing aid that didn't work, and I wore it to Christmas dinner, and I said, what? to everything. And for one whole Christmas, my family talked right in front of me, as if I wasn't there. What I heard changed my will, my house, and my heart.""",
   "elderly woman with hearing aid at Christmas dinner", "I PRETENDED TO BE DEAF")
sc("""Tonight I'm going to tell you why I did it, the three things I heard at that table, and the one person who didn't change at all when they thought I couldn't hear. Stay with me, honey, because I was wrong about almost everybody. And the person I was most wrong about is the reason I'm telling this story.""",
   "family whispering across Christmas table", "I WAS WRONG")

ch("Why I did it")
sc("""It started with something my late husband said, the year before he died. Harold said, Ruthie, people don't change when you can't hear them. They just stop pretending. He'd lost most of his hearing in the plant, and he told me the things people said in front of him, thinking he couldn't hear, would curl my hair. Some of them were about me, honey. Kind things. And some weren't.""",
   "elderly man with old hearing aid thoughtful", "THEY STOP PRETENDING")
sc("""After Harold passed, I had the house, a little savings, and five grandchildren. And everybody was so sweet to me, sweetheart. Phone calls on Sundays. Flowers on my birthday. But I'd started to notice little things. A look between my daughter-in-law and my son. The way conversations stopped when I walked into the kitchen. And I wanted to know the truth while I was still alive to do something about it.""",
   "conversation stopping when woman enters kitchen", "CONVERSATIONS STOPPED")
sc("""So in November, I told everybody the doctor said my hearing was going. I ordered a cheap hearing aid off the television for nineteen dollars, the kind that just makes a little whistling noise. I practiced saying, what, dear? in front of the mirror. And I started tilting my head and leaning in. In a minute I'll tell you who saw through it in about thirty seconds. It wasn't who you'd think.""",
   "cheap hearing aid in package", "NINETEEN DOLLARS")

sc("""My friend Dottie told me I was crazy, honey. She said, Ruth, you'll hear things you can never un-hear. And some of them won't even be true. People say terrible things when they're tired and scared. I said, Dottie, I'm seventy-two. I don't have time to wonder anymore. I'd rather know. She shook her head and said, then God help you. And she was right to worry. She usually was.""",
   "two elderly friends arguing over coffee", "YOU CAN'T UN-HEAR IT")
ch("Christmas dinner")
sc("""On Christmas Day, all of them came. My son Mark and his wife, Denise. My daughter Susan and her husband, Tom. And five grandchildren from nine to twenty-three. Fourteen of us around my table, the way it had been every Christmas for fifty years. And at first, honey, everybody shouted at me. MERRY CHRISTMAS, MOM! DO YOU WANT MORE GRAVY? I just smiled and nodded.""",
   "large family around Christmas dinner table", "EVERYBODY SHOUTED")
sc("""But by the second helping, they forgot about me. They started talking to each other across the table as if I were a lamp. And the first thing I heard, sweetheart, was Denise, my son's wife, leaning over to Susan. She said, have you seen the price of houses on this street? This place has to be worth three hundred thousand now. And Susan said, Mark already talked to a realtor. Didn't he tell you?""",
   "two women whispering at dinner table", "THREE HUNDRED THOUSAND")

ch("What I heard")
sc("""If you're enjoying Grandma's story, tap the like button for me, honey. It helps more people find these stories. The second thing I heard was about where I'd go. My son Mark said, there's a nice place out on Route Thirty. Sunny Acres. Mom wouldn't even notice the difference, the way she's going. And Tom said, does she have long-term care insurance? Because if not, that'll eat through the house money in two years.""",
   "son talking across dinner table", "SHE WOULDN'T NOTICE")
sc("""I sat there, sweetheart, buttering my roll, my heart pounding so hard I was sure they could hear it. My own son. The boy I'd rocked through croup, and driven to football practice at five in the morning, and paid for college with my egg money. Talking about my house like it was a used car. I kept smiling. I said, what, dear? when Susan asked me to pass the salt. And she laughed.""",
   "elderly woman buttering roll trying to smile", "MY OWN SON")
sc("""And the third thing I heard, the worst one, was my oldest grandson, Kyle, twenty-three, who I'd always thought was my sweet boy. He was on his phone, and he said to his cousin, Grandma's getting so weird. I hate coming here. It smells like old people. I only came because Mom said she'd pay my car insurance. And they both laughed. And something in me cracked right down the middle.""",
   "young man on phone laughing at table", "I HATE COMING HERE")

sc("""And the strangest part, sweetheart, was how normal it all sounded to them. Nobody whispered. Nobody lowered their voice. They talked about my house and my money and my smell right over my head, while they ate my turkey and my stuffing and my mother's rolls, the same way you'd talk about the weather. That's what hurt the most. Not that they said it. That it was so easy.""",
   "family eating and talking casually at dinner", "IT WAS SO EASY")
ch("Lily")
sc("""So far, the house, the nursing home, and Kyle. And then there was Lily. Lily was my youngest granddaughter, Susan's girl, nine years old. Quiet, funny little thing, with a gap in her teeth. She was sitting next to me, and halfway through dinner, she leaned over and looked at my hearing aid, very closely. Then she looked at my face. And then she whispered, so quietly nobody else could hear, Grandma, that's not turned on.""",
   "little girl whispering to grandmother at table", "THAT'S NOT TURNED ON")
sc("""I looked at her, honey. And she looked at me. And she smiled, that gap-toothed smile, and she whispered, I won't tell. And then for the rest of that dinner, every time somebody said something unkind, Lily would reach under the table and squeeze my hand. Every single time. Nine years old. She knew exactly what I was hearing, and she sat there holding on to me.""",
   "child's hand holding grandmother's hand under table", "SHE SQUEEZED MY HAND")
sc("""After dinner, while the others watched football, Lily came and found me in the kitchen doing the dishes. She picked up a towel and started drying, without being asked. And she said, Grandma, why are you pretending? And I told her the truth, sweetheart. I said, because I wanted to know who loves me when they think I'm not listening. And she said, oh, Grandma. I could have just told you that.""",
   "grandmother and granddaughter doing dishes", "I COULD HAVE TOLD YOU")

ch("What Lily told me")
sc("""And then she told me, honey. Things I never knew. That her mother cried in the car every time they left my house, because she missed her father so much and my house reminded her of him. That Mark had been laid off in October and hadn't told me, and that's why he'd called the realtor, because he was scared and ashamed. And that Kyle was failing out of college and wouldn't tell anybody.""",
   "little girl telling grandmother secrets kitchen", "THINGS I NEVER KNEW")
sc("""I stood at that sink with my hands in the dishwater, and I realized, sweetheart, that I'd only heard half of it. Harold was right. People stop pretending when they think you can't hear. But what you hear isn't the whole truth either. You hear the fear, and the meanness, and the tiredness. You don't hear the crying in the car. It took a nine-year-old to tell me the rest.""",
   "elderly woman at kitchen sink thinking", "I ONLY HEARD HALF")

ch("New Year's Day")
sc("""On New Year's Day, I called everybody back to the house. And I took out that hearing aid, and I put it on the table, and I said, I owe you all an apology. I can hear perfectly. I've heard every word since Thanksgiving. Fourteen faces went white, honey. Denise dropped her coffee cup. Kyle got up and walked out to his car. And Mark put his face in his hands.""",
   "hearing aid placed on dining table", "I CAN HEAR PERFECTLY")
sc("""Then I said, Mark, why didn't you tell me you lost your job? And my son, fifty years old, started to cry at my dining room table, the way he cried when he was six. He said, Mom, I didn't want you to worry. I thought if we sold the house and got you somewhere safe, I could help everybody at once. I didn't mean it the way it sounded. I'm so sorry.""",
   "grown man crying at dining table", "I'M SO SORRY")
sc("""And Kyle came back in from the car after twenty minutes, red-eyed, and he stood in the doorway and said, Grandma, I'm failing. I'm failing everything, and I'm scared, and I didn't know how to tell anybody. So I just acted like a jerk. He was twenty-three, honey. And he put his head on my shoulder like he used to when he was little, and I held him.""",
   "young man hugging grandmother in doorway", "I'M FAILING")

sc("""And then my daughter Susan stood up and said, Mom, I need to tell you something too. And she told me about crying in the car. Every time. For four years. She said, I can't stand being in this house without Daddy, so I stopped coming as much, and then I felt guilty, and the guilt made me short with you. And we stood in the middle of my dining room and held each other, and cried for Harold together.""",
   "mother and daughter embracing in dining room", "WE CRIED FOR HAROLD")
ch("What I did")
sc("""Here's what I did with the truth, sweetheart. I didn't sell the house. But I invited Mark and Denise to move in for a year while he found new work, and he did, by June. I paid for Kyle to go to the community college and live in my spare room, and he helped me with the yard. He graduated two years later. He brings me lunch every Tuesday. And he says my house smells like Christmas.""",
   "young man helping grandmother in garden", "IT SMELLS LIKE CHRISTMAS")
sc("""And Lily? Lily is nineteen now, honey. She's studying to be an audiologist, a hearing doctor. She says it's because of me. And every Christmas, she gives me the same present. That broken hearing aid, wrapped in new paper, with a little card that says, Grandma, I'm still listening. I have ten of those cards in a box on my dresser.""",
   "small gift box with old hearing aid and card", "I'M STILL LISTENING")

ch("Grandma's lesson")
sc("""Here's what I want you to remember, honey. If you go looking for what people really think of you, you'll find it. But you'll only find half. The other half is fear, and money trouble, and shame, and love that doesn't know how to come out right. Don't stop at the half that hurts. Find your Lily. Ask the one who's quiet. They always know the rest.""",
   "grandmother and young woman hugging", "FIND YOUR LILY")
sc("""Tell me in the comments, sweetheart. Was I wrong to pretend? Be honest with Grandma. And who's the quiet one in your family who sees everything? There are more of my family's stories in the playlist right there on your screen, and I'll see you in the next one. Good night, honey.""",
   "cozy living room Christmas evening lamp", "WAS I WRONG?")

for s in S:
    s["cuts"], s["moves"] = 4, True

